WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.280
In 1910 a white-eyed male fruit fly turned up in a laboratory full of red-eyed

2
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:15.880
flies. Its crosses gave different answers, depending on which parent was white.

3
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.660
Mendel's peas never did this.

4
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.440
So why would the parent matter here?

5
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:24.653
The answer is the X chromosome.

6
00:00:25.473 --> 00:00:27.393
A female fly is XX.

7
00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:35.613
A male fly is XY. The white gene sits on the X, and the Y carries no copy.

8
00:00:36.193 --> 00:00:38.073
So a male is hemizygous.

9
00:00:38.553 --> 00:00:40.353
He has one copy, not two.

10
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:43.633
And he gets his only X from his mother.

11
00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:49.613
Write X+ for the red allele, and Xw for white.

12
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:56.813
Cross a red female, X+X+, to a white male, XwY.

13
00:00:57.753 --> 00:01:00.353
Every daughter is X+Xw.

14
00:01:01.153 --> 00:01:05.633
Every son is X+Y. All of them have red eyes.

15
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.733
Now cross those flies to each other.

16
00:01:09.493 --> 00:01:13.113
The daughters all get X+ from their father, so they are red.

17
00:01:13.113 --> 00:01:17.353
The sons get their X from the mother, so half are white.

18
00:01:18.213 --> 00:01:21.233
Overall, three red flies to one white.

19
00:01:22.053 --> 00:01:23.653
Every white fly is a male.

20
00:01:24.433 --> 00:01:26.253
Now reverse the cross.

21
00:01:27.073 --> 00:01:33.393
A white female, XwXw, meets a red male, X+Y.

22
00:01:34.173 --> 00:01:37.593
The daughters are X+Xw, and red.

23
00:01:38.393 --> 00:01:41.353
The sons are XwY, and white.

24
00:01:42.173 --> 00:01:44.213
The father's eyes pass to his daughters.

25
00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:46.473
The mother's pass to her sons.

26
00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:49.493
This is called criss-cross inheritance.

27
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:53.867
Now cross those F1 flies to each other.

28
00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:58.387
The mother makes eggs with X+ or Xw.

29
00:01:59.407 --> 00:02:06.707
The father makes sperm with Xw or Y. Each of the four combinations has probability

30
00:02:06.707 --> 00:02:14.227
one quarter. That gives one red female, one white female, one red male, and one

31
00:02:14.227 --> 00:02:19.247
white male. Among 400 flies, expect 100 of each.

32
00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:25.007
An autosomal gene would give the same first generation in both crosses.

33
00:02:26.047 --> 00:02:31.747
These crosses differ, and the difference follows the X. Sons always take the

34
00:02:31.747 --> 00:02:37.587
mother's allele. So the gene is on the X. Mendel's segregation still holds.

35
00:02:38.287 --> 00:02:40.687
Real broods are noisier than these expectations.
